    archLegendSolid: 'load-bearing',
    archLegendDashed: 'optional: cut it, every unit keeps its mission',
    archCaption: "Only the mesh is load-bearing. The operator link and the C2 sit beside it: cut either one and the swarm keeps flying the mission it already has.",
    pillars: [
      { title: 'Goals, not commands', body: 'A mission crosses the mesh once, as desired end states. Each unit bids for work, plans its own steps and replans when the world changes. There is no central coordinator to lose.' },
      { title: 'A mesh that never syncs', body: 'No requests, no acknowledgements, no sessions: each unit streams what it knows and decodes whatever it hears. A unit back from behind a hill decodes its way back in.', more: 'The swarm that never syncs', href: '/notes/the-swarm-that-never-syncs' },
      { title: 'Safety below the mission', body: 'Battery and geofence return-to-home, separation between aircraft and the takeoff GO / NO-GO run natively, underneath any mission, and are enforced again at the autopilot link.' },
    ],

    statusTag: 'STATUS',
    statusTitle: 'Where it stands',
    statusBody: "Running today on ESP32-P4 boards. On the bench, a P4 drives a PX4 autopilot over UART (PX4 in software-in-the-loop on a Raspberry Pi, physics simulated), meshes over its encrypted radio with a second P4 in the ground role, and flies missions through it. It has not flown a real airframe yet. Everything on this page outside the items marked design, pending or in design runs today.",

    videoTag: 'FILM',
    videoLabel: 'The SPU in under five minutes',
    videoCaption: "What it is, Loom and the Mission Studio, the ground station, then recorded scenario runs replayed in its tactical map. In the flight footage, positions, goals and claims are recorded; the camera, trails and markers are staged; synthetic flight model where noted.",

    detailsTag: 'DETAILS',
    detailsTitle: 'The full specification',
    detailsLede: 'Everything else, folded away. Open what you need.',
    specsTitle: 'Everything it does today',
    specs: [
      "Zephyr RTOS firmware, one image: drone or ground-control role set at configuration time",
      "Decentralized coordination runtime (fleece): replicated shared state plus goal-oriented mission planning, running on every unit",
      "Its own mesh over any transparent serial radio, every frame sealed with XChaCha20-Poly1305 under a provisioned fleet key",
      "MAVLink to PX4 with streamed position control and commanded speed; INAV flight controllers over MSP behind the same interface",
      "Payload control: gimbal pointing, camera capture and zoom, and a release, over MAVLink; plus a companion-computer channel whose observations feed on-board sensor fusion",
      "CBBA-style auction over shared state: each unit publishes its own bid, the highest live bid wins; a unit that falls silent stops counting and its work is taken over",
      "Time-critical goals: a goal with a deadline the unit cannot physically meet is declined at claim time, not accepted and missed",
      "Native safety layer below the mission: battery and geofence return-to-home, altitude bands, a predicted-closest-approach guard between aircraft, planned radio silence per unit",
      "Takeoff GO / NO-GO: a grounded unit asks before it leaves the ground; one operator's GO launches it, any NO-GO vetoes it, and arm and takeoff are refused again at the autopilot link without a valid GO",
      "Operator cost zones (keep-out, keep-in, altitude bands, time windows, per-capability exceptions) priced into the auction along each leg; units route around hard zones and decline work that ends inside one, saying which zone",
      "The mission vocabulary is provisioned data, identified by its hash: update it from the setup port or by dropping a file on the board's USB drive, with the built-in vocabulary as fallback",
      "Witnessed completion: a step marked for verification counts only once a different unit has re-observed it",
      "A provisioned airframe envelope (speeds, climb, sink, turn radius) so a unit refuses, with the arithmetic, what its aircraft cannot fly",
      "The ground role exposes a separate operator link: swarm management software never touches the mesh wire format",
    ],
    decideTitle: 'How a unit decides',
    decideBody: "Goals and actions are data, not hand-wired control flow. Each goal carries a satisfied-when condition and a score; each action a precondition and an effect. A bounded receding-horizon planner searches for a path to the best achievable goal and replans in place the moment the world stops matching the plan: a target found, a cell already claimed, a link degraded. The search runs under a hard per-tick budget, so its cost stays bounded on microcontroller-class hardware. New mission behaviour means a new goal or action, never a change to the planner or the firmware.",
    takTitle: 'TAK, in detail',
    tak: [
      "In: contacts from TAK peers on the local network (SA multicast) or a TAK Server, in CoT XML or TAK protocol v1, drawn with their MIL-STD-2525 symbol",
      "Promote: one click turns a contact into a point of interest the whole swarm shares; nothing an external C2 sends reaches the radio by itself",
      "Out: every unit as a friendly UAV track, the swarm's points of interest, and its fused contacts with their uncertainty",
      "Not yet: TLS to a TAK Server, chat and data packages",
    ],
    designTitle: 'In design: C2 adapters and linked swarms',
    design: [
      "Tasking from a C2 system arrives as goals, checked against the unit's provisioned vocabulary, never executed as a raw command; who may task what is explicit per link, and every tasking is logged with its origin",
      "Adapters are modules bound to a serial or Ethernet link, so a new C2 dialect is a module, not a firmware release",
      "Swarms with no radio path between them share their picture through a gateway unit over IP, cellular or satellite, each keeping its own key and its own allocation",
      "Built for slow, metered, intermittent links: changes only, rate-capped; a swarm that loses the link keeps coordinating and re-converges when it returns",
    ],
    designNote: "These are designs, not products: no date, no price, and nothing else on this page depends on them.",
    trademarks: 'PX4, INAV, TAK, ATAK, WinTAK and iTAK are names and trademarks of their respective owners, used here only to say what the SPU works with. It does not imply endorsement.',

